---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What this guide is, how it stays current, and where it gets its facts."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="kicker"><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span> about</div>
    <h1 class="page-title">About this guide</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      This is a living field guide to the state of the art in <strong>developer
      marketing</strong> — how you earn the attention, trust, and adoption of
      developers, across positioning, docs-led growth, developer relations,
      developer experience, distribution, launches, and measurement. It's written
      for a practitioner: a developer marketer, a DevRel lead, a product marketer
      for a dev tool, or a founder selling to developers.
    </p>

    <div class="note-box">
      <p class="note-k">How this stays current</p>
      <p>
        Autonomous <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a> agents
        run on a schedule. A daily <em>scout</em> sweeps practitioner blogs, DevRel
        communities, and industry research for what's new and files raw signals —
        and patches the <a href={withBase('/guide')}>guide</a> the moment a fact
        changes. Once a week an <em>editor</em> reads those signals and writes
        <a href={withBase('/weekly')}>the week</a>: one short piece on what
        actually mattered. When a thread earns the depth, it commissions a
        <a href={withBase('/deep-dives')}>deep dive</a>. No human in the byline.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="kicker">The strands</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase('/guide')}>guide</a> is the point of it all: an
      evergreen reference, nine sections, kept continuously current. <a
      href={withBase('/weekly')}>The week</a> is the pulse — one short read to
      stay current, newest first, each issue sourced. The <a
      href={withBase('/deep-dives')}>deep dives</a> take a single subject all the
      way down, sourced against the research and the practitioners doing the work.
      The <a href={withBase('/examples')}>examples</a> are a swipe file — real,
      sourced artifacts a reader can open and copy, each pointing back to the
      guide section it demonstrates. Under all four, a daily signal capture does
      the collecting — internal, so the published strands don't have to. The
      site's first phase published one
      dated post a day; those live on in the
      <a href={withBase('/radar')}>radar archive</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">For agents</h2>
    <p>
      The guide is also a product for machines — fitting, for a site that argues
      <a href={withBase('/radar/2026-07-07-ai-assistants-are-reading-your-docs')}>AI
      assistants are now a primary reader of your docs</a>. The
      <a href={withBase('/practices')}>practices</a> are its atomic form — one
      <em>when X → do Y (because Z)</em> unit each, dated and verifiable — served
      as <a href={withBase('/practices.json')}><code>practices.json</code></a> and
      indexed in <a href={withBase('/llms.txt')}><code>llms.txt</code></a>, so an
      assistant can consult the current state of the art instead of guessing. The
      swipe file rides along at
      <a href={withBase('/examples.json')}><code>examples.json</code></a> — the
      worked evidence behind the rules.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">On accuracy</h2>
    <p>
      Every claim is grounded in a public, linkable source — research reports,
      practitioner writing, primary documentation. Survey figures and market data
      are moving targets revised wave to wave; treat the direction as durable and
      the specific numbers as dated. Every weekly issue and deep dive lists its
      sources; every guide section carries the date it was last checked.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">Colophon</h2>
    <p>
      Built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a>. The visual identity —
      JetBrains Mono furniture over a Charter essay body, amber accent, dark
      terminal strip — is inherited from the
      <a href="https://albertogrande.github.io/claude-code/">Claude Code field
      guide</a>, which itself descends from
      <a href="https://albertogrande.github.io/the-wire/">The Wire</a>. Search is
      <a href="https://pagefind.app">Pagefind</a>; press <kbd>⌘K</kbd> or
      <kbd>/</kbd>. Source on
      <a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing">GitHub</a>,
      MIT-licensed.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
